1
00:00:03,799 --> 00:00:09,318
When I reached home, my sister was very curious to know all about Miss Havisham’s,

2
00:00:09,318 --> 00:00:14,959
and asked a number of questions. And I soon found myself getting heavily bumped from behind

3
00:00:14,959 --> 00:00:20,159
in the nape of the neck and the small of the back, and having my face ignominiously shoved

4
00:00:20,159 --> 00:00:25,439
against the kitchen wall, because I did not answer those questions at sufficient length.

5
00:00:25,439 --> 00:00:29,638
If a dread of not being understood be hidden in the breasts of other young people to

6
00:00:29,639 --> 00:00:34,760
anything like the extent to which it used to be hidden in mine,—which I consider probable,

7
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:40,680
as I have no particular reason to suspect myself of having been a monstrosity,—it is the key to

8
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:47,920
many reservations. I felt convinced that if I described Miss Havisham’s as my eyes had seen it,

9
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:53,478
I should not be understood. Not only that, but I felt convinced that Miss Havisham

10
00:00:53,478 --> 00:00:59,599
too would not be understood; and although she was perfectly incomprehensible to me,

11
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:03,680
I entertained an impression that there would be something coarse and treacherous in my dragging

12
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:10,920
her as she really was (to say nothing of Miss Estella) before the contemplation of Mrs. Joe.

13
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:17,279
Consequently, I said as little as I could, and had my face shoved against the kitchen wall.

14
00:01:17,279 --> 00:01:22,040
The worst of it was that that bullying old Pumblechook, preyed upon by a devouring

15
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:27,519
curiosity to be informed of all I had seen and heard, came gaping over in his chaise-cart at

16
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:32,879
tea-time, to have the details divulged to him. And the mere sight of the torment,

17
00:01:32,879 --> 00:01:37,799
with his fishy eyes and mouth open, his sandy hair inquisitively on end,

18
00:01:37,799 --> 00:01:43,879
and his waistcoat heaving with windy arithmetic, made me vicious in my reticence.

19
00:01:43,879 --> 00:01:47,520
“Well, boy,” Uncle Pumblechook began, as soon as

20
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:52,639
he was seated in the chair of honour by the fire. “How did you get on up town?”

21
00:01:52,639 --> 00:01:57,719
I answered, “Pretty well, sir,” and my sister shook her fist at me.

22
00:01:57,719 --> 00:02:01,000
“Pretty well?” Mr. Pumblechook repeated. “Pretty

23
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,239
well is no answer. Tell us what you mean by pretty well, boy?”

24
00:02:06,239 --> 00:02:12,638
Whitewash on the forehead hardens the brain into a state of obstinacy perhaps. Anyhow,

25
00:02:12,639 --> 00:02:18,559
with whitewash from the wall on my forehead, my obstinacy was adamantine. I reflected

26
00:02:18,559 --> 00:02:25,239
for some time, and then answered as if I had discovered a new idea, “I mean pretty well.”

27
00:02:25,239 --> 00:02:30,439
My sister with an exclamation of impatience was going to fly at me,—I had no shadow of defence,

28
00:02:30,439 --> 00:02:35,120
for Joe was busy in the forge,—when Mr. Pumblechook interposed with “No! Don’t

29
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:40,680
lose your temper. Leave this lad to me, ma’am; leave this lad to me.” Mr.

30
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:45,478
Pumblechook then turned me towards him, as if he were going to cut my hair, and said,—

31
00:02:45,478 --> 00:02:49,679
“First (to get our thoughts in order): Forty-three pence?”

32
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:55,318
I calculated the consequences of replying “Four Hundred Pound,” and finding them against me,

33
00:02:55,318 --> 00:03:00,439
went as near the answer as I could—which was somewhere about eightpence off. Mr.

34
00:03:00,439 --> 00:03:05,279
Pumblechook then put me through my pence-table from “twelve pence make one shilling,” up to

35
00:03:05,279 --> 00:03:10,519
“forty pence make three and fourpence,” and then triumphantly demanded, as if he had done for me,

36
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:16,559
“Now! How much is forty-three pence?” To which I replied, after a long interval of reflection,

37
00:03:16,559 --> 00:03:22,318
“I don’t know.” And I was so aggravated that I almost doubt if I did know.

38
00:03:22,318 --> 00:03:26,040
Mr. Pumblechook worked his head like a screw to screw it out of me,

39
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:31,879
and said, “Is forty-three pence seven and sixpence three fardens, for instance?”

40
00:03:31,879 --> 00:03:37,000
“Yes!” said I. And although my sister instantly boxed my ears, it was highly

41
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,520
gratifying to me to see that the answer spoilt his joke, and brought him to a dead stop.

42
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:48,680
“Boy! What like is Miss Havisham?” Mr. Pumblechook began again when he had recovered;

43
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:52,680
folding his arms tight on his chest and applying the screw.

44
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:55,879
“Very tall and dark,” I told him.

45
00:03:55,879 --> 00:03:58,840
“Is she, uncle?” asked my sister.

46
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:03,000
Mr. Pumblechook winked assent; from which I at once inferred that he had

47
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,359
never seen Miss Havisham, for she was nothing of the kind.

48
00:04:07,359 --> 00:04:11,200
“Good!” said Mr. Pumblechook conceitedly. (“This is the

49
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:15,439
way to have him! We are beginning to hold our own, I think, Mum?”)

50
00:04:15,439 --> 00:04:20,200
“I am sure, uncle,” returned Mrs. Joe, “I wish you had him always;

51
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:22,840
you know so well how to deal with him.”

52
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:28,439
“Now, boy! What was she a-doing of, when you went in today?” asked Mr. Pumblechook.

53
00:04:28,439 --> 00:04:32,959
“She was sitting,” I answered, “in a black velvet coach.”

54
00:04:32,959 --> 00:04:35,719
Mr. Pumblechook and Mrs. Joe stared at one

55
00:04:35,719 --> 00:04:41,840
another—as they well might—and both repeated, “In a black velvet coach?”

56
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:47,439
“Yes,” said I. “And Miss Estella—that’s her niece, I think—handed her in cake and wine

57
00:04:47,439 --> 00:04:52,000
at the coach-window, on a gold plate. And we all had cake and wine on gold

58
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,040
plates. And I got up behind the coach to eat mine, because she told me to.”

59
00:04:57,719 --> 00:05:01,279
“Was anybody else there?” asked Mr. Pumblechook.

60
00:05:01,279 --> 00:05:03,398
“Four dogs,” said I.

61
00:05:03,398 --> 00:05:05,478
“Large or small?”

62
00:05:05,478 --> 00:05:11,558
“Immense,” said I. “And they fought for veal-cutlets out of a silver basket.”

63
00:05:11,559 --> 00:05:16,879
Mr. Pumblechook and Mrs. Joe stared at one another again, in utter amazement. I

64
00:05:16,879 --> 00:05:23,959
was perfectly frantic,—a reckless witness under the torture,—and would have told them anything.

65
00:05:23,959 --> 00:05:29,120
“Where was this coach, in the name of gracious?” asked my sister.

66
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:34,000
“In Miss Havisham’s room.” They stared again. “But there weren’t any horses to

67
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,359
it.” I added this saving clause, in the moment of rejecting four richly

68
00:05:39,359 --> 00:05:44,120
caparisoned coursers which I had had wild thoughts of harnessing.

69
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:50,159
“Can this be possible, uncle?” asked Mrs. Joe. “What can the boy mean?”

70
00:05:50,159 --> 00:05:57,239
“I’ll tell you, Mum,” said Mr. Pumblechook. “My opinion is, it’s a sedan-chair. She’s flighty,

71
00:05:57,239 --> 00:06:04,000
you know,—very flighty,—quite flighty enough to pass her days in a sedan-chair.”

72
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,719
“Did you ever see her in it, uncle?” asked Mrs. Joe.

73
00:06:08,719 --> 00:06:11,760
“How could I,” he returned, forced to the admission,

74
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:16,920
“when I never see her in my life? Never clapped eyes upon her!”

75
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:21,760
“Goodness, uncle! And yet you have spoken to her?”

76
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:26,680
“Why, don’t you know,” said Mr. Pumblechook, testily, “that when I have been there,

77
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:31,120
I have been took up to the outside of her door, and the door has stood ajar,

78
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:36,398
and she has spoke to me that way. Don’t say you don’t know that, Mum. Howsever,

79
00:06:36,398 --> 00:06:40,879
the boy went there to play. What did you play at, boy?”

80
00:06:40,879 --> 00:06:46,879
“We played with flags,” I said. (I beg to observe that I think of myself with amazement,

81
00:06:46,879 --> 00:06:50,639
when I recall the lies I told on this occasion.)

82
00:06:50,639 --> 00:06:53,680
“Flags!” echoed my sister.

83
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:57,239
“Yes,” said I. “Estella waved a blue flag,

84
00:06:57,239 --> 00:07:02,679
and I waved a red one, and Miss Havisham waved one sprinkled all over with little gold stars,

85
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:08,600
out at the coach-window. And then we all waved our swords and hurrahed.”

86
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:13,799
“Swords!” repeated my sister. “Where did you get swords from?”

87
00:07:13,799 --> 00:07:20,200
“Out of a cupboard,” said I. “And I saw pistols in it,—and jam,—and pills. And

88
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:24,799
there was no daylight in the room, but it was all lighted up with candles.”

89
00:07:24,799 --> 00:07:30,680
“That’s true, Mum,” said Mr. Pumblechook, with a grave nod. “That’s the state of the case,

90
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:35,639
for that much I’ve seen myself.” And then they both stared at me,

91
00:07:35,639 --> 00:07:39,840
and I, with an obtrusive show of artlessness on my countenance,

92
00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:45,840
stared at them, and plaited the right leg of my trousers with my right hand.

93
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:50,879
If they had asked me any more questions, I should undoubtedly have betrayed myself,

94
00:07:50,879 --> 00:07:55,000
for I was even then on the point of mentioning that there was a balloon in the yard,

95
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:00,359
and should have hazarded the statement but for my invention being divided between that phenomenon

96
00:08:00,359 --> 00:08:06,359
and a bear in the brewery. They were so much occupied, however, in discussing the marvels

97
00:08:06,359 --> 00:08:12,638
I had already presented for their consideration, that I escaped. The subject still held them when

98
00:08:12,639 --> 00:08:18,398
Joe came in from his work to have a cup of tea. To whom my sister, more for the relief

99
00:08:18,398 --> 00:08:24,838
of her own mind than for the gratification of his, related my pretended experiences.

100
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:30,600
Now, when I saw Joe open his blue eyes and roll them all round the kitchen in helpless amazement,

101
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:36,478
I was overtaken by penitence; but only as regarded him,—not in the least as

102
00:08:36,478 --> 00:08:43,558
regarded the other two. Towards Joe, and Joe only, I considered myself a young monster,

103
00:08:43,559 --> 00:08:47,639
while they sat debating what results would come to me from Miss Havisham’s acquaintance

104
00:08:47,639 --> 00:08:53,719
and favour. They had no doubt that Miss Havisham would “do something” for me; their doubts related

105
00:08:53,719 --> 00:09:00,000
to the form that something would take. My sister stood out for “property.” Mr. Pumblechook was in

106
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:05,318
favour of a handsome premium for binding me apprentice to some genteel trade,—say,

107
00:09:05,318 --> 00:09:10,920
the corn and seed trade, for instance. Joe fell into the deepest disgrace with both,

108
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:15,439
for offering the bright suggestion that I might only be presented with one of the dogs who had

109
00:09:15,439 --> 00:09:21,279
fought for the veal-cutlets. “If a fool’s head can’t express better opinions than that,” said

110
00:09:21,279 --> 00:09:28,279
my sister, “and you have got any work to do, you had better go and do it.” So he went.

111
00:09:28,279 --> 00:09:32,879
After Mr. Pumblechook had driven off, and when my sister was washing up,

112
00:09:32,879 --> 00:09:40,000
I stole into the forge to Joe, and remained by him until he had done for the night. Then I said,

113
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,719
“Before the fire goes out, Joe, I should like to tell you something.”

114
00:09:44,719 --> 00:09:46,279
“Should you, Pip?” said Joe,

115
00:09:46,279 --> 00:09:51,679
drawing his shoeing-stool near the forge. “Then tell us. What is it, Pip?”

116
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:54,760
“Joe,” said I, taking hold of his rolled-up shirt sleeve,

117
00:09:54,760 --> 00:10:00,799
and twisting it between my finger and thumb, “you remember all that about Miss Havisham’s?”

118
00:10:00,799 --> 00:10:05,920
“Remember?” said Joe. “I believe you! Wonderful!”

119
00:10:05,920 --> 00:10:09,520
“It’s a terrible thing, Joe; it ain’t true.”

120
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:12,600
“What are you telling of, Pip?” cried Joe, falling back in the

121
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:15,799
greatest amazement. “You don’t mean to say it’s—”

122
00:10:15,799 --> 00:10:17,920
“Yes I do; it’s lies, Joe.”

123
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:23,000
“But not all of it? Why sure you don’t mean to say, Pip, that there was no black welwet

124
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:29,279
co—eh?” For, I stood shaking my head. “But at least there was dogs, Pip? Come,

125
00:10:29,279 --> 00:10:35,040
Pip,” said Joe, persuasively, “if there warn’t no weal-cutlets, at least there was dogs?”

126
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:36,478
“No, Joe.”

127
00:10:36,478 --> 00:10:41,000
“A dog?” said Joe. “A puppy? Come?”

128
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,359
“No, Joe, there was nothing at all of the kind.”

129
00:10:44,359 --> 00:10:49,478
As I fixed my eyes hopelessly on Joe, Joe contemplated me in dismay. “Pip,

130
00:10:49,478 --> 00:10:55,000
old chap! This won’t do, old fellow! I say! Where do you expect to go to?”

131
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:56,959
“It’s terrible, Joe; ain’t it?”

132
00:10:56,959 --> 00:11:01,920
“Terrible?” cried Joe. “Awful! What possessed you?”

133
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:06,799
“I don’t know what possessed me, Joe,” I replied, letting his shirt sleeve go,

134
00:11:06,799 --> 00:11:10,318
and sitting down in the ashes at his feet, hanging my head;

135
00:11:10,318 --> 00:11:14,519
“but I wish you hadn’t taught me to call Knaves at cards Jacks; and I wish my

136
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:21,600
boots weren’t so thick nor my hands so coarse.” And then I told Joe that I felt very miserable,

137
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:26,959
and that I hadn’t been able to explain myself to Mrs. Joe and Pumblechook, who were so rude to me,

138
00:11:26,959 --> 00:11:32,040
and that there had been a beautiful young lady at Miss Havisham’s who was dreadfully proud,

139
00:11:32,040 --> 00:11:37,318
and that she had said I was common, and that I knew I was common, and that I wished I was

140
00:11:37,318 --> 00:11:43,079
not common, and that the lies had come of it somehow, though I didn’t know how.

141
00:11:43,079 --> 00:11:48,840
This was a case of metaphysics, at least as difficult for Joe to deal with as for me. But

142
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:56,398
Joe took the case altogether out of the region of metaphysics, and by that means vanquished it.

143
00:11:56,398 --> 00:12:02,959
“There’s one thing you may be sure of, Pip,” said Joe, after some rumination, “namely, that lies is

144
00:12:02,959 --> 00:12:08,040
lies. Howsever they come, they didn’t ought to come, and they come from the father of lies,

145
00:12:08,040 --> 00:12:13,398
and work round to the same. Don’t you tell no more of ’em, Pip. That ain’t the way to get

146
00:12:13,398 --> 00:12:19,398
out of being common, old chap. And as to being common, I don’t make it out at all clear. You

147
00:12:19,398 --> 00:12:26,079
are oncommon in some things. You’re oncommon small. Likewise you’re a oncommon scholar.”

148
00:12:26,079 --> 00:12:29,680
“No, I am ignorant and backward, Joe.”

149
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:34,760
“Why, see what a letter you wrote last night! Wrote in print even! I’ve seen

150
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:40,200
letters—Ah! and from gentlefolks!—that I’ll swear weren’t wrote in print,” said Joe.

151
00:12:40,200 --> 00:12:45,959
“I have learnt next to nothing, Joe. You think much of me. It’s only that.”

152
00:12:45,959 --> 00:12:50,719
“Well, Pip,” said Joe, “be it so or be it son’t, you must be a common

153
00:12:50,719 --> 00:12:56,600
scholar afore you can be a oncommon one, I should hope! The king upon his throne,

154
00:12:56,600 --> 00:13:00,439
with his crown upon his ed, can’t sit and write his acts of Parliament in print,

155
00:13:00,439 --> 00:13:06,879
without having begun, when he were a unpromoted Prince, with the alphabet.—Ah!” added Joe,

156
00:13:06,879 --> 00:13:11,239
with a shake of the head that was full of meaning, “and begun at A too,

157
00:13:11,239 --> 00:13:18,519
and worked his way to Z. And I know what that is to do, though I can’t say I’ve exactly done it.”

158
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:23,120
There was some hope in this piece of wisdom, and it rather encouraged me.

159
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:27,680
“Whether common ones as to callings and earnings,” pursued Joe, reflectively,

160
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:31,920
“mightn’t be the better of continuing for to keep company with common ones, instead of going

161
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:38,120
out to play with oncommon ones,—which reminds me to hope that there were a flag, perhaps?”

162
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:39,840
“No, Joe.”

163
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:46,200
“(I’m sorry there weren’t a flag, Pip). Whether that might be or mightn’t be, is a thing as can’t

164
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:51,279
be looked into now, without putting your sister on the Rampage; and that’s a thing not to be thought

165
00:13:51,279 --> 00:13:57,558
of as being done intentional. Lookee here, Pip, at what is said to you by a true friend. Which this

166
00:13:57,559 --> 00:14:02,959
to you the true friend say. If you can’t get to be oncommon through going straight, you’ll never

167
00:14:02,959 --> 00:14:09,879
get to do it through going crooked. So don’t tell no more on ’em, Pip, and live well and die happy.”

168
00:14:09,879 --> 00:14:12,600
“You are not angry with me, Joe?”

169
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:19,000
“No, old chap. But bearing in mind that them were which I meantersay of a stunning and outdacious

170
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:24,680
sort,—alluding to them which bordered on weal-cutlets and dog-fighting,—a sincere

171
00:14:24,680 --> 00:14:28,359
well-wisher would adwise, Pip, their being dropped into your meditations,

172
00:14:28,359 --> 00:14:34,599
when you go upstairs to bed. That’s all, old chap, and don’t never do it no more.”

173
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:40,398
When I got up to my little room and said my prayers, I did not forget Joe’s recommendation,

174
00:14:40,398 --> 00:14:45,558
and yet my young mind was in that disturbed and unthankful state, that I thought long after I

175
00:14:45,559 --> 00:14:53,079
laid me down, how common Estella would consider Joe, a mere blacksmith; how thick his boots,

176
00:14:53,079 --> 00:14:59,959
and how coarse his hands. I thought how Joe and my sister were then sitting in the kitchen, and how I

177
00:14:59,959 --> 00:15:06,359
had come up to bed from the kitchen, and how Miss Havisham and Estella never sat in a kitchen, but

178
00:15:06,359 --> 00:15:12,558
were far above the level of such common doings. I fell asleep recalling what I “used to do” when I

179
00:15:12,559 --> 00:15:18,559
was at Miss Havisham’s; as though I had been there weeks or months, instead of hours; and as though

180
00:15:18,559 --> 00:15:24,559
it were quite an old subject of remembrance, instead of one that had arisen only that day.

181
00:15:24,559 --> 00:15:29,719
That was a memorable day to me, for it made great changes in me. But it is the same with

182
00:15:29,719 --> 00:15:35,719
any life. Imagine one selected day struck out of it, and think how different its course would

183
00:15:35,719 --> 00:15:43,478
have been. Pause you who read this, and think for a moment of the long chain of iron or gold,

184
00:15:43,478 --> 00:15:49,679
of thorns or flowers, that would never have bound you, but for the formation of the first link on

185
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:51,984
one memorable day. Chapter 10.
